.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
.TH FPAL 8 "November 2026" "fpal @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
fpal \- fingerprint sensor tool: list, name, bind, enrol and delete fingers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fpal
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fpal
talks to a Match-on-Chip fingerprint sensor (EgisTec / LighTuning, for example
USB id 1c7a:0587) through usbdevfs, without libusb or libfprint. The sensor
stores the fingerprints and does the matching itself.
.B fpal
lists them, gives them names, binds them to local users, and can store a new
fingerprint or delete one.
.PP
Only root can open the sensor, so run it with
.BR sudo (8).
Fingerprints enrolled in Windows Hello are recognised as they are.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info
Show the sensor, driver, serial number and firmware version.
.TP
.B list
List the fingerprints on the sensor: number, id and name.
.TP
.BR identify " [" \-t " \fIms\fR]"
Wait for a finger and show which fingerprint matched.
.TP
.BR bind " [" \-t " \fIms\fR] \fIuser\fR"
Bind the touched finger to a local user, so that
.BR pam_fpal (8)
accepts it for that user.
.TP
.BI unbind " user"
Forget the bindings of a user. Only the file on the computer is removed; the
sensor is not touched.
.TP
.BI bindings " user"
Show the fingerprints bound to a user.
.TP
.BR label " \fIfinger\fR [\fIname\fR|" \-\-clear ]
Show, set or remove the name of a finger. The sensor cannot store a name, so
names are kept on this computer and are not visible to other systems. A name is
1 to 48 bytes (UTF-8 is fine) without control characters or leading or trailing
space.
.TP
.BR enroll " [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \-\-allow\-chip\-write "] [" \-\-name " \fIname\fR] \fIuser\fR"
Enrol a NEW finger: one touch to check that the finger is not enrolled yet,
then 20 scans, then the template is stored on the sensor and bound to
.IR user .
Existing fingerprints are never changed.
.B \-\-dry\-run
goes through every step except the commands that store the template and
checks that the sensor's list did not change.
.TP
.BR delete " [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \-\-allow\-chip\-write "] [" \-\-allow\-foreign "] \fIfinger\fR"
Delete ONE fingerprint from the sensor. This cannot be undone. See
.BR SAFETY .
.TP
.BR status " [\fIuser\fR]"
Say whether fingerprint login is set up: the sensor, the fingerprints and the
user's bindings (run it with
.BR sudo (8)
to read those), whether
.B pam_fpal.so
is in the PAM stack, whether
.BR fpald (8)
answers for GNOME, and whether
.B fprintd
is masked. Anything missing is listed with what to do about it. The exit
status is 1 if something is missing.
.TP
.BR setup " [" \-\-revert ]
Make
.BR sudo (8),
.BR su (1)
and the GNOME login and lock screens use the fingerprint: select the
authselect profile with
.BR pam_fpal ,
mask
.BR fprintd ,
and start
.BR fpald (8).
.B \-\-revert
goes back. It runs
.IR authselect\-fpal.sh ,
which is installed next to
.BR fpal ,
and needs root. Only authselect systems (Fedora, RHEL) are supported so far.
.TP
.B version
Show the version.
.PP
A
.I finger
is its number in
.BR "fpal list" ,
its exact name, or the start of its id (at least 8 hex digits, or 6 characters
of text such as
.BR FPAL\-7b51 ).
If that matches more than one finger, nothing happens.
.SH SAFETY
Reading, naming and binding never change what the sensor stores.
.B enroll
and
.B delete
do, so they are guarded:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-allow\-chip\-write
is required (or
.BR \-\-dry\-run ),
and you must type
.B yes
at a terminal. Nothing can be confirmed from a script.
.IP \(bu 2
.B delete
handles exactly one fingerprint. There is no command to delete several or to
clear the sensor. Afterwards the sensor's list is read back and the command
fails if anything other than that one fingerprint changed.
.IP \(bu 2
A fingerprint that
.B fpal
did not create (its id does not start with
.BR FPAL\- ,
so it most likely comes from Windows Hello) is refused unless
.B \-\-allow\-foreign
is given, and then you must type
.B DELETE
instead of
.BR yes .
Deleting it removes it for every system that uses the sensor.
.IP \(bu 2
A deleted fingerprint is removed from every user's bindings and its name is
forgotten.
.PP
Binaries built with
.B make ENROLL=0
cannot enrol or delete at all. The PAM module and
.BR fpald (8)
never can, whatever
.B fpal
is built with.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/fpal/USER
Fingerprints bound to a user (root only, mode 0600).
.TP
.I /var/lib/fpal/.labels
Names of fingerprints (root only, mode 0600).
.TP
.I /run/fpal.lock
Keeps two processes from using the sensor at once.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FPAL_TRACE=1
Print every USB packet exchanged with the sensor, as hex.
.TP
.B FPAL_DEBUG=1
Verbose messages.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error or a refused operation, 2 on a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo fpal list
sudo fpal label 0 "Windows finger"
sudo fpal setup
fpal status
sudo fpal bind $USER
sudo fpal enroll \-\-dry\-run $USER
sudo fpal enroll \-\-allow\-chip\-write \-\-name "Linux finger" $USER
sudo fpal delete \-\-allow\-chip\-write "Linux finger"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_fpal (8),
.BR fpald (8),
.UR https://github.com/ohNando/fpal
.UE
